From dad1c15a00e1c5a4277862023ecd5dbf3e51713a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:47:29 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] ipa: softisp: af: Measure the focus with the focus
 statistics

Feed the libipa Af algorithm with a focus measurement computed from the
focus statistics of the software ISP, in place of the variance
placeholder, which is removed from the statistics.

The measurement covers the centre half of the width and third of the
height of the statistics window, the default focus area of the Raspberry
Pi autofocus algorithm. It is the mean squared difference between
adjacent green values, normalised by the square of the mean level above
black, so that exposure changes don't look like focus changes. Images
too dark to measure are skipped.

The software ISP computes statistics for one frame in
SwStatsCpu::kStatPerNumFrames, and the statistics of the other frames
carry the values of an earlier frame. Skip them, as the other algorithms
do: the hill climbing scan would otherwise move the lens on every frame
and compare each position with a measurement made at an earlier one. In
a simulation of the scan, with the lens moving two frames after a
command, it settled 90 to 190 lens steps past the peak, and didn't
settle at all when the statistics were processed three or four frames
after the capture, as happens when debayering takes most of the frame
time. Skipping them, it settled within 20 steps of the peak.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
